1
00:00:00,048 --> 00:00:03,558
So the setup is the first important step.

2
00:00:03,783 --> 00:00:06,460
The base usage we also kind of saw already,

3
00:00:06,540 --> 00:00:08,255
though there's a bit more to that. But

4
00:00:08,287 --> 00:00:11,621
what's also important is configuring Claude

5
00:00:11,637 --> 00:00:14,507
Code or knowing how to configure it. And

6
00:00:14,779 --> 00:00:15,982
regarding that,

7
00:00:16,527 --> 00:00:19,476
you got a couple of options. You can

8
00:00:19,588 --> 00:00:22,586
configure Claude Code globally on your

9
00:00:22,634 --> 00:00:23,644
system. Actually,

10
00:00:24,221 --> 00:00:26,401
as an enterprise, you can even configure it

11
00:00:26,401 --> 00:00:28,950
for all your team members. But here we'll

12
00:00:28,950 --> 00:00:31,306
focus on individual installations on a

13
00:00:31,306 --> 00:00:33,599
system. Well, then you can configure it

14
00:00:33,599 --> 00:00:36,019
globally. For example, here on Mac, I have a

15
00:00:36,099 --> 00:00:38,905
global.claude folder on my system in my

16
00:00:39,129 --> 00:00:41,902
current user's home directory. In there is a

17
00:00:41,950 --> 00:00:43,826
bunch of stuff, but one of the things in

18
00:00:43,858 --> 00:00:46,391
there is a settings.json file. And when you

19
00:00:46,663 --> 00:00:48,715
open that settings file, you'll be able to

20
00:00:48,763 --> 00:00:51,969
set up some global settings. You can also

21
00:00:51,969 --> 00:00:54,502
tweak those settings though from inside

22
00:00:54,534 --> 00:00:55,223
Claude code.

23
00:00:55,816 --> 00:00:56,249
If you run

24
00:00:57,163 --> 00:01:00,016
/config, it'll bring up that configuration

25
00:01:00,145 --> 00:01:02,774
menu where you can browse through the

26
00:01:02,790 --> 00:01:05,245
various settings it has and the current

27
00:01:05,277 --> 00:01:07,814
values and also change those values.

28
00:01:08,392 --> 00:01:10,897
For example, if you wanna change that theme

29
00:01:10,897 --> 00:01:12,904
you initially picked, you can select that

30
00:01:12,904 --> 00:01:15,055
here, bring up that theme picker in this

31
00:01:15,071 --> 00:01:17,223
case, and choose a different theme, though

32
00:01:17,255 --> 00:01:19,310
I'll stay with the default dark mode here.

33
00:01:19,808 --> 00:01:22,104
And you can do that for all these settings.

34
00:01:22,393 --> 00:01:24,448
For example, if you wanna save tokens and

35
00:01:24,448 --> 00:01:26,311
you know you don't have complex tasks, you

36
00:01:26,311 --> 00:01:28,607
could turn off thinking mode and set this to

37
00:01:28,623 --> 00:01:29,008
false.

38
00:01:29,746 --> 00:01:31,654
And you'll see that now, for example, in

39
00:01:31,766 --> 00:01:34,026
this global settings file here,

40
00:01:34,732 --> 00:01:38,050
this alwaysThinkingEnabled value here

41
00:01:38,579 --> 00:01:41,064
changed to false and is now set to false

42
00:01:41,064 --> 00:01:44,831
because I made this change in my Claude CLI.

43
00:01:45,296 --> 00:01:47,636
Because whenever you change a config there,

44
00:01:48,021 --> 00:01:49,880
it's by default, at least right now when I'm

45
00:01:49,880 --> 00:01:52,653
recording this, editing the global settings.

46
00:01:52,990 --> 00:01:55,859
Now, Claude Code has a long list of

47
00:01:55,907 --> 00:01:57,607
available settings, which of course also

48
00:01:57,655 --> 00:01:59,723
changes over time as features are added,

49
00:01:59,739 --> 00:02:01,904
changed, and so on. Attached. You therefore

50
00:02:01,904 --> 00:02:03,749
find a link to the official documentation so

51
00:02:03,749 --> 00:02:05,900
that you can see the most recent version of

52
00:02:05,900 --> 00:02:08,580
that list. One setting which I find

53
00:02:08,580 --> 00:02:11,453
important, which is not set by default, at

54
00:02:11,453 --> 00:02:13,989
least right now when I'm recording this, is

55
00:02:14,326 --> 00:02:16,653
this permissions setting here. Though

56
00:02:17,536 --> 00:02:21,773
here you can allow or deny certain things,

57
00:02:22,351 --> 00:02:25,224
and you wanna deny Claude Code

58
00:02:25,817 --> 00:02:28,672
reading your.env files, for example, where

59
00:02:28,769 --> 00:02:30,660
you might be keeping secrets or

60
00:02:31,814 --> 00:02:33,818
other kind of sensitive information you

61
00:02:33,914 --> 00:02:36,832
don't want Claude Code to read. And the way

62
00:02:37,104 --> 00:02:40,887
this permissions system works is such that

63
00:02:40,887 --> 00:02:43,773
you specify the tool Claude Code may be

64
00:02:43,773 --> 00:02:46,145
using, and there are various built-in tools,

65
00:02:46,642 --> 00:02:48,822
but the most important ones for this context

66
00:02:48,886 --> 00:02:52,221
are read and write and bash, which allow

67
00:02:52,333 --> 00:02:54,465
Claude, you guessed it, to read or write or

68
00:02:54,657 --> 00:02:56,485
execute bash commands.

69
00:02:57,142 --> 00:02:58,873
And you can then specify

70
00:02:59,403 --> 00:03:01,683
some restrictions essentially related to

71
00:03:01,731 --> 00:03:04,107
which files you want to deny using these

72
00:03:04,139 --> 00:03:05,761
tools. Because of course I don't want to

73
00:03:05,890 --> 00:03:08,796
generally deny Claude Code from using the

74
00:03:08,860 --> 00:03:11,509
read or write tools, but I want to deny it

75
00:03:11,766 --> 00:03:13,998
using these tools on any files stored

76
00:03:14,094 --> 00:03:16,021
anywhere that are called

77
00:03:16,567 --> 00:03:17,081
.env. And

78
00:03:17,611 --> 00:03:20,485
that is in the end what this rule setup here

79
00:03:20,597 --> 00:03:22,893
should achieve. Now I'll switch always

80
00:03:23,070 --> 00:03:24,852
thinking enabled back to true here, by the

81
00:03:24,852 --> 00:03:27,389
way, because I want it to use thinking mode.

82
00:03:27,983 --> 00:03:30,324
Another place where settings can be stored

83
00:03:30,693 --> 00:03:32,987
is inside of your project folder. So here in

84
00:03:33,035 --> 00:03:35,665
this next folder, in there you can add a

85
00:03:35,986 --> 00:03:39,450
.claud folder and in there you can also add

86
00:03:39,450 --> 00:03:42,241
a settings.json file. And then in here you

87
00:03:42,241 --> 00:03:44,037
can set up Claude settings that will

88
00:03:44,246 --> 00:03:46,604
override the global settings for this

89
00:03:46,812 --> 00:03:49,074
project. You can also add a

90
00:03:49,074 --> 00:03:52,506
settings.local.json file, which will also be

91
00:03:52,506 --> 00:03:54,414
a project-specific settings file that will

92
00:03:54,591 --> 00:03:57,863
override the global settings and that will

93
00:03:58,039 --> 00:03:59,851
also override the settings from this

94
00:03:59,899 --> 00:04:00,909
settings.json file.

95
00:04:01,567 --> 00:04:04,357
And the idea behind settings.local is simply

96
00:04:04,469 --> 00:04:06,891
that it's not checked into source control.

97
00:04:06,971 --> 00:04:10,868
So it's not part of your GitHub repository,

98
00:04:10,884 --> 00:04:13,273
for example, so that individual users, if

99
00:04:13,273 --> 00:04:14,636
you're in a team and you're working on a

100
00:04:14,636 --> 00:04:16,448
project, each user can have their own

101
00:04:16,496 --> 00:04:16,897
settings

102
00:04:17,442 --> 00:04:19,784
that extend or override parts of the

103
00:04:19,784 --> 00:04:21,756
project-specific or global settings.
